Jerusalem, and there is no one to bury the dead.","verse":3}],"id":"Jerusalem","label":"Jerusalem","matchReviews":[{"ruleId":"Jerusalem::Jerusalem::reviewed_unambiguous_name","scope":"reviewed_unambiguous_name","senseReview":"Reviewed complete original name headword and source subject. Article covers the biblical person(s), named place, or named spiritual figure denoted by this exact spelling; multiple persons under one unchanged source article remain together. Actual proper-name capitalization and whole-name boundaries required. Explicit occurrence-level ambiguity exclusions override this rule.","sourceHeadwordId":"Jerusalem","term":"Jerusalem"}],"matchingRule":"reviewed_exact_headword","matchingRules":["reviewed_exact_headword"],"sourceProvenanceId":"Jerusalem","sourceTitle":"Jerusalem"},{"evidence":[{"matchingRule":"reviewed_exact_headword","phrase":"LORD","senseEvidence":{"basis":"reviewed_capitalized_divine_title","originalCitations":[],"ruleId":"Lord::LORD::reviewed_capitalized_divine_title","sourceHeadwordId":"Lord"},"text":"How long, O LORD? Will You be angry forever? Will Your jealousy burn like fire?","verse":5}],"id":"Lord","label":"LORD","matchReviews":[{"ruleId":"Lord::LORD::reviewed_capitalized_divine_title","scope":"reviewed_capitalized_divine_title","senseReview":"Only literal uppercase LORD is admitted globally. Source Lord article explicitly covers God as Lord; lowercase human lord and sentence-title Lord are not inferred from this rule.","sourceHeadwordId":"Lord","term":"LORD"}],"matchingRule":"reviewed_exact_headword","matchingRules":["reviewed_exact_headword"],"sourceProvenanceId":"Lord","sourceTitle":"Lord"}],"format":"bsb-study-chapter-v2","notes":[{"chapterAssociationStatus":"canonical_reference","content":[{"c":[{"c":[{"href":"?bref=Ps.79.1-13","r":"Ps.79.1-13","rawR":"Ps.79.1-13","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Ps.79.1-13","value":"Ps.79.1-13"}],"t":"ref","v":"Ps 79"}],"sourceClass":"sn-ref","t":"span"}," This lament describes the occasion of Jerusalem’s fall in 586 ",{"c":["BC"],"sourceClass":"era","t":"span"},". The early history of Israel’s rebellions described in ",{"href":"?bref=Ps.78.1-72","r":"Ps.78.1-72","rawR":"Ps.78.1-72","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Ps.78.1-72","value":"Ps.78.1-72"}],"t":"ref","v":"Ps 78"}," provoked God’s wrath, resulting in the desolation of Zion described here as the defilement of the Temple and the disgraced bodies that littered the landscape. The psalmist then prays for forgiveness and rescue (",{"href":"?bref=Ps.79.8-9","r":"Ps.79.8-9","rawR":"Ps.79.8-9","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Ps.79.8-9","value":"Ps.79.8-9"}],"t":"ref","v":"79:8-9"},"), as well as for God to pay back the nation’s enemies (",{"href":"?bref=Ps.79.12-13","r":"Ps.79.12-13","rawR":"Ps.79.12-13","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Ps.79.12-13","value":"Ps.79.12-13"}],"t":"ref","v":"79:12-13"},"). Other psalms lamenting the Exile include ",{"href":"?bref=Ps.42.1-44.26","r":"Ps.42.1-44.26","rawR":"Ps.42.1-44.26","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Ps.42.1-44.26","value":"Ps.42.1-44.26"}],"t":"ref","v":"Pss 42–44"},", ",{"href":"?bref=Ps.102.1-28","r":"Ps.102.1-28","rawR":"Ps.102.1-28","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Ps.102.1-28","value":"Ps.102.1-28"}],"t":"ref","v":"102"},", ",{"href":"?bref=Ps.107.1-43","r":"Ps.107.1-43","rawR":"Ps.107.1-43","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Ps.107.1-43","value":"Ps.107.1-43"}],"t":"ref","v":"107"},", ",{"href":"?bref=Ps.126.1-6","r":"Ps.126.1-6","rawR":"Ps.126.1-6","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Ps.126.1-6","value":"Ps.126.1-6"}],"t":"ref","v":"126"},", ",{"href":"?bref=Ps.137.1-9","r":"Ps.137.1-9","rawR":"Ps.137.1-9","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Ps.137.1-9","value":"Ps.137.1-9"}],"t":"ref","v":"137"},"."],"sourceClass":"sn-text","t":"p"}],"id":"StudyNote:Ps.79.1-13","refs":[{"book":"PSA","endChapter":79,"endVerse":13,"startChapter":79,"startVerse":1}],"sourceAttributes":{"name":"Ps.79.1-13","product":"TyndaleOpenStudyNotes","typename":"StudyNote"},"sourceBookAppCode":"PSA","sourceChapterCoverage":[79],"sourceItemFragmentSha256":"065109cbdd64c6628d26fad05